  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 AM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nVery early this morning, an outflow from last night's convection \nover Mexico drifted northeastward across the Brush Country, bringing \na surprising scattered coverage of showers and a few thunderstorms. \nAs of writing, the outflow is just now making it to Duval and \nMcMullen counties with activity seeming to wane as the atmosphere \nbecomes more stable. \n\nModel guidance has underestimated precip coverage over the Brush \nCountry the past few days. Therefore, I've increased rain chances to \nat least 30% instead of the less than 15% NBM wanted to put in for \nthis afternoon. Duval County has notoriously been the sweet spot for \nstrong storms to develop along the seabreeze the past couple of \ndays, something to watch out for if the pattern continues. Moisture \nwill remain above normal through this week, around or greater than \n2.0\" PWATs, as the mid-level ridge to our north flattens over the \nsouthern CONUS and South Texas remains on the southern periphery. \nWeak mid-level disturbances will occasionally propagate their way \nwestward across the Gulf into South Texas, increasing rain chances \nmidweek. Most convection will be attributed to the afternoon \nseabreeze, but strong thunderstorms able to produce significant \noutflows can act as an additional lifting mechanism for popcorn \nshowers and storms to develop.\n\nNo relief in sight for the above normal heat, with apparent \ntemperatures 105-110 and air temperatures reaching the triple digits \nover the Brush Country.",
